The folk of Greyhollow live their lives against stone — houses cut into the mountain's flank, chimneys coughing wood-smoke against a sky already threatening the first snows of Leaffall. Deep in the mountain, under her anvil-shaped gate of worked iron, stands the Temple of the Anvil Father, and for six years you stood guard at its doors, Brakka Ironvein, until the god put a different road under your boots.

Three days ago, three miners went down into the old north workings, the tunnels sealed a generation back after a collapse no one much likes to speak of. None came up. Last night a fourth man — Drus, one of the lads who'd worked the timber framing — staggered out of the dark alone, babbling about lights that walk, and died before dawn with the prior Hessa at his side.

The council means to brick that entrance shut at sunrise tomorrow, bodies unburied or not. Hessa could not order you to go. She only asked, in the way a dwarf asks when she knows the answer already. Now it is late afternoon, the light going grey and low, and you stand at the mine mouth: a black, propped arch of timber in the hillside north of town, the temple's hooded lantern warm in your hand, the wind finding every crack in your chain.

The entrance yawns before you, silent and cold. What do you do?


>>>>>> round 1, the player: I check my gear before going in: shield on my arm, axe loose in its loop, and the lantern's hood adjusted to a narrow beam. Then I crouch at the mouth and look for tracks in the dust and mud, to see whether the three miners' boots and Drus's stumbling return are still readable.

