  [dice Brakka's Wisdom (Survival) check to read the tracks at the mine mouth, DC 13: 1d20+4 = 1d20 [7] + 4 = 11]

You go over your kit the way a guard does before a long watch: shield settled on your arm, your waraxe's haft loose in its loop, the hood of the lantern drawn to a narrow finger of light. Then you crouch and study the ground at the mouth.

The trampled dust tells you a little, but not much you don't already know. There has been traffic through here — the council's men came to look the place over yesterday, and their boots have churned the apron of the entrance into a muddle. Under and through it you can just make out the mark of three pairs of boots going in, one set much heavier, the steady stride of miners carrying their kit. The way back out is worse: one stumbling, dragging track, heels scuffed and splayed as if the man was leaning on the wall every other step. That will be Drus, and it looks as if he was alone. Past the first ten paces, the tracks vanish into the dark where the light and the clean air end.

The wind pushes at your back, cold and smelling of wet stone and old timbers.


>>>>>> round 2, the player: I nod to myself and step into the tunnel, keeping the shield up and the lantern's narrow beam low on the floor. I walk slowly, watching the timber props overhead and the ground ahead for the miners' tracks, and I whisper a short prayer to the Anvil Father as I go.

  [time: 20 minutes later: Day 1, 4:40 PM]

You pass under the propped arch and the wind is behind you no longer. The air inside is still and heavy, and the lantern's narrow beam picks out the floor in a moving stripe of gold: packed earth first, then a scatter of fallen rock that rings dully under your boots.

You walk slowly, shield up, eyes moving between the floor and the roof. The old timbers are bowed in places and furred with white rot, but they hold; here and there a prop has snapped and been shored with newer wood — the council's men again, you'd guess. The tracks thin as the dust does, and before long the floor is pocked bare stone with only a stray scuff to mark that anyone ever walked here.

The tunnel runs straight for a good while, then turns and begins to slope downward in long, low steps cut by pick and chisel. A cold draught comes up from below, and with it the faint, sour whisper of old air escaping somewhere deeper. You murmur your prayer as you go; the metal of the lantern is warm in your hand, and the dark closes quietly in behind you.


>>>>>> round 3, the player: I stop at the top of the stepped slope and kneel to check the first few steps for any sign of the miners' passage: scuffs, dropped tools, a scrap of cloth. I also listen for a moment, holding my breath, for any sound from below before I start down.

  [dice Brakka's Wisdom (Survival) check for signs of the miners' passage on the steps, DC 11: 1d20+4 = 1d20 [2] + 4 = 6]
  [dice Brakka's Wisdom (Perception) check to listen for sounds from below, DC 13: 1d20+4 = 1d20 [15] + 4 = 19]

You kneel at the head of the steps and run the light over them. The stone here is hard and dry, and it keeps no print that you can pick out of the shadow — what looked like scuffs turn out to be old chisel-marks and the scrape of timbers dragged down years ago. Nothing dropped, nothing torn; no scrap that isn't older than you are.

So you hold your breath and listen instead. At first there is only the small sound of your own blood and the rustle of your mail. Then, very far below, you catch it: a thin, irregular noise, like a stone shifting, or perhaps water finding a way through — three soft knocks, then silence, too faint to place and too deliberate-feeling to ignore. It does not come again.

The steps descend into the dark, and the draught from below is steadier now, colder against your face.


>>>>>> round 4, the player: I wait a few breaths more to see if the knocking repeats, then start down the steps one at a time, shield forward and the lantern low. I keep my weight on my back foot and test each step before trusting it, ready to stop the moment I hear it again.

